hi I'm Matt Welch for recent TV I am  here at Freedom Fest in Las Vegas  honored to be joined by the great John  Mackey of Whole Foods you've been  interested in libertarian causes and  thought for a long time you've also been  part of this incredible kind of  revolution in the way that we talk and  think and interact with food over the  years and and the politics of food I  think have become pretty interesting in  this country as part of that be adjusted  to get in your take it seems to me and  maybe I'm wrong that a lot of the same  kind of energy or the same kind of  people who've been interested in the  sourcing of food and and and being more  conscious about where we where we get  and how we process food particularly  some of that energy then goes into kind  of restricting the choices or banning  certain practices it doesn't have to  lead to that but there seems to be  something in human nature that wants to  restrict other people's choices some  people are seem to not be happy unless  they're telling other people what to do  that they know best and that so that's  always discouraging do you or Whole  Foods get involved in these kind of  fights as they play out politically or  are you more interested in in the  consciousness-raising aspect of it  we're more interested in the  consciousness-raising aspects of it  we've occasionally waded into certain  political battles like we've been  involved in getting organic standards  into law and and we felt like there were  inconsistency wasn't a level playing  field of people were cheating so we  thought having consistent organic  definitions and everybody playing by  those rules would probably be a good  idea and I think on balance it has been  a good idea but of course there problems  with it and  Organic itself is highly politicized  Whole Foods is you very frequently  criticized most recently the New York  Times for being a you know one of the  big corporations that is corrupting  organic and just because we're large a  lot of suspicion and mistrust is because  there's such an anti corporation  mentality by a lot of people in the  country uh and I mean just because  you're successful corporation is it that  they they're charging that that you are  kind of a bogus you're well the next you  have to understand the narrative that  many people have about business and  capitalism is that they're fundamentally  selfish and greedy and exploitative in  corporations are sociopaths that they  don't care about anything or anyone but  themselves and make it as much money as  possible so therefore they cannot be  trusted and they become too powerful and  then they they bribe people and they  they sell poisonous things and they're  fundamentally evil and of course I don't  agree with that narrative and in fact  you have a book coming out later this  year talking about this tell us a little  bit about it what's the title  well capitalism of course itself has  been amazing it's been a first chapter  in the book is extraordinary success or  capitalism the second chapter is why  isn't capitalism better left and we put  forth a thesis that it we need a new  narrative for we need to think about  business differently that business asks  the possibility to have a higher purpose  besides just just making money there's  nothing wrong with making money but  that's not particularly inspiring and  every other profession in the world from  doctors teachers engineers architects  lawyers if you ask what their purpose is  it refers back to some type of  contribution they're making to other  people and business is the greatest  value creator in the world business  creates value for its customers for its  employees for suppliers for its  investors for the larger communities  it's part of their the value creators we  are the value creators we're the heroes  and yet that's not the narrative this  told about business so we're telling  that NER  and at the same time we're challenging  business to be more conscious and to  take their value creation to a higher  level and understanding that all of  these stakeholders they're creating  value for connected together and so you  ought to strategically organize your  business in such a way that you can  optimize value for all of those  stakeholders Fred Smith of the committed  Enterprise Institute frequently kind of  bemoans the fact that capitalists and  entrepreneurs are no longer selling that  story that heroic story about themselves  and are not interested in in  contributing to the discussion over all  of the benefits of capitalism in the  same way that you know we might remember  them doing in the 1950s and 1960s there  seem to be more kind of let's just talk  about the underlying principles here do  you share that kind of critique out  there or what's what's your take on the  I we have a section in the book where we  talk about how the intellectuals hijack  the narrative of capitalism in business  and you can go back to I mean Adam Smith  wrote two great books for wealth of  nations and the Theory of Moral  Sentiments he synthesized together those  together and you have a of self-interest  and you have empathy and care and  sympathy for others and you have the  foundation for a great economic system  but intellectuals and economists ignored  that ethical foundation of capitalism  and they tried to ground it on strict  self-interest and that led to ethical  attacks  I mean Marxism fundamentally is an  ethical attack on capitalism is  fundamentally wrong and evil and that's  it and then the economists even the  defenders of capitalism played into  trying to defend it strictly on the  basis of the decree it creates wealth  and but not its ethical foundation of it  unless you do a Randian type of ethical  foundation that it is about  self-interest and that's great  unfortunately that hasn't sold very well  to most people it and so we're trying to  change that narrative and  get people to realize the type of value  that business creates so the narrative  of business being fundamentally greedy  and selfish and exploitative that  undermines business's standing in  society and it can't be defended it has  to be defended on an ethical basis not  just on the fact that it creates wealth  that's great thank you john mack from  Whole Foods Matt Welch from recent TV
 

